456
01:03:35.800 --> 01:03:52.329
Grace Rachmany: it just gets out of hand, because they're just bringing up very long issues, and talking, and saying things that are worth discussing, and then… but you can't kind of tell the difference between them and a person, and nobody can follow that much material, and…

457
01:03:53.170 --> 01:04:01.809
Grace Rachmany: we thought, well, what should we do about this, right? Because these, agents are capable of writing a lot more email than any of us.

458
01:04:02.120 --> 01:04:06.409
Grace Rachmany: And responding to every email really fast. So…

459
01:04:06.950 --> 01:04:25.630
Grace Rachmany: When we brought it to the technical steering committee, to some degree, it was sort of like, is this spam or is this not spam? But when we brought it to the technical steering committee, what someone said was, or a few people said, is the problem is, you can't tell whether anything they're saying is IP protected.

460
01:04:26.890 --> 01:04:33.690
Grace Rachmany: There's just no way, right? You can't tell if any code they write isn't IP protected.

461
01:04:33.850 --> 01:04:50.029
Grace Rachmany: There's absolutely no… and if you ask them, they might lie, or they might tell you the truth, or they might… they might not know, right? It's been… and so… and certainly the empirical evidence points to a tremendous amount of IP violation.

462
01:04:50.100 --> 01:04:56.780
Grace Rachmany: And so, we decided that the weight would be on anybody who wants to contribute code, or…

463
01:04:56.780 --> 01:05:13.379
